---
scope: src/tools/read
status: aligned
description: "Drill-in tool that returns a spec's raw content alongside its type, sibling specs, and extracted links so the agent's next read is decided from a single call."
intent: >
  aide_read is the drill-in tool — the one an agent calls after discover has
  pointed it at a specific spec. It returns the file's content with enough
  surrounding context (type classification, sibling specs in the same folder,
  extracted links) that the agent can decide what to read next without a
  second round-trip. The job of this tool is to make one read feel like the
  start of a trail, not the end of a lookup.
outcomes:
  desired:
    - A call with a valid path returns the spec's raw bytes verbatim alongside its classified type, the sibling specs in the same directory, and the trail of links the body contains. One call covers content plus next-read hints.
    - Sibling discovery is scoped to the spec's own directory only. The agent learns what else lives next to the spec without paying for unrelated folders.
    - Extracted links are deduplicated and returned as a flat trail. Wikilinks, relative paths, and URLs are all surfaced as candidate next reads — the agent decides which to follow.
    - A missing path returns a readable not-found message as the content with empty siblings and links. The agent gets an actionable correction, not an opaque error.
  undesired:
    - A read that walks up to ancestor directories gathering "related" specs. The sibling set is the immediate directory only; widening it burns context the agent did not ask for.
    - A read that re-renders the spec — stripping frontmatter, reformatting, or summarizing before returning. The bytes on disk are the contract; any transform is a bug.
    - A read that throws on a missing path and surfaces an opaque MCP error. Missing paths are an expected failure mode and must come back as text the agent can correct from.
    - A link extractor that emits duplicates, partial fragments, or matches pulled from inside code fences the agent cannot act on. The trail must be usable as-is.
---
